---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TrimAudio from '@/components/tools/media/TrimAudio.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="trim-audio">
  <TrimAudio client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to trim an audio file</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in an MP3, WAV, M4A, FLAC, OGG or Opus file. It loads into a player at once.</li>
      <li>Drag the two handles to the section you want to keep, or play the track and press <strong>Set here</strong> to use the playhead position.</li>
      <li>Type exact timecodes such as <code>0:45</code>, <code>1:23.75</code> or <code>1:02:30</code> for precision to the hundredth of a second.</li>
      <li>Keep <strong>Lossless</strong> mode unless you want a fade or a different output format.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Trim audio</strong>, check the preview, and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Lossless audio trimming and its limits</h2>
    <p>
      In copy mode the encoded audio passes through unchanged, without decoding or re-encoding, so there is no
      generation loss. Compressed audio is stored in frames, so a cut lands on the nearest frame boundary, not
      on the exact sample.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Frame length</th>
            <th>Worst-case cut error</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MP3 at 44.1 kHz</td>
            <td>1152 samples</td>
            <td>≈ 26 ms</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AAC (M4A)</td>
            <td>1024 samples</td>
            <td>≈ 23 ms</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Opus</td>
            <td>20 ms default</td>
            <td>≈ 20 ms</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WAV / FLAC</td>
            <td>Sample accurate</td>
            <td>None</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      About 20 milliseconds is a fiftieth of a second, which you cannot hear in speech or music. It matters only
      when you cut to a beat grid; then re-encode mode gives a sample-exact edit.
    </p>

    <h2>Fades</h2>
    <p>
      A fade changes the samples, so a stream copy cannot do it. When you set a fade length, the audio trimmer
      changes to re-encoding and tells you. A 0.5-1 second fade in and out removes the click of a cut in the
      middle of a waveform. Add one to almost any clip from the middle of a recording.
    </p>

    <h2>Making a ringtone or an alert sound</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Trim the section you want. 20-30 seconds is the usual maximum for a ringtone.</li>
      <li>Add a short fade out so it does not stop abruptly if it plays to the end.</li>
      <li>Convert to the format your phone needs: Android takes MP3, and iPhone needs M4A (an M4R is an AAC file with a different extension).</li>
      <li>Copy it to the device. The original recording stays on your computer.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why there is no waveform</h2>
    <p>
      A waveform needs the whole file decoded to PCM first. For a two-hour recording that means a long wait and
      much memory, and on a phone it may not finish. This tool uses the browser's own player for instant
      scrubbing, and the "Set here" buttons copy the playhead into the start or end field to the hundredth of a
      second.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
